dh_virtualenv installs from PyPI -- and, for whatsmyname itself, from a pinned
commit archive on GitHub -- during the build, so this package cannot be built
in sbuild's default offline chroot. build/build-package.sh passes
--enable-network when this marker is present.

This is a deliberate, narrow exception to Debian's "builds must be offline"
rule, and it is per-package rather than global so that tlosint-meta and
tlosint-config keep the stronger guarantee.

What keeps it honest: requirements.txt is fully hash-pinned and carries a
--require-hashes directive, and that covers the GitHub archive exactly as it
covers the PyPI wheels -- pip verifies the tarball's sha256 before unpacking
it. The build can only ever install the exact artifacts we locked. Network
access cannot change what lands in the package, only whether the build
succeeds at all. debian/upstream.pin records where the archive URL and its
hash came from.

The alternative -- vendoring wheels into the source package -- was rejected:
it would mean committing ~40 MB of binaries to main, which handoff section 8
forbids, and the wheels would have to be downloaded with the chroot's exact
interpreter and platform tags rather than the host's.

Separate from the build: whatsmyname also needs network at RUNTIME. It fetches
the WhatsMyName site list (wmn-data.json) from GitHub on every invocation,
before it parses its arguments, so even "whatsmyname --help" fails without a
network. Nothing in this package can change that short of patching upstream.
